{
  "section": "deep-policy-gradient-actor-critic-ppo",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "trajectory-objective-score-function",
      "title": "Trajectory objectives and the likelihood-ratio identity",
      "hours": 4,
      "target": [
        "derive"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "rl-4-trajectory-objectives-and-expected-return",
        "rl-4-likelihood-ratio-and-score-function-identities"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Factor a trajectory likelihood and identify which factors depend on policy parameters.",
        "Derive the score-function estimator from expected return, with its support and differentiation assumptions.",
        "Check the estimator against an exactly enumerated policy objective."
      ],
      "why": "The environment need not be differentiable to optimize a stochastic policy. This identity supplies the gradient used by REINFORCE and later language-policy updates.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "reinforcement-learning/deep-policy-gradient-actor-critic-ppo/policy-gradient-theorem-reinforce",
          "note": "Turn the trajectory estimator into an implementable update."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "rl4-berkeley-cs285-policy-gradients-actor-critic",
          "title": "CS 185/285 Lecture 5: Policy Gradients",
          "url": "https://rail.eecs.berkeley.edu/deeprlcourse/static/slides/lec-5.pdf",
          "locator": "Slides 3 to 7: objective, trajectory factorization, direct differentiation and the sampled gradient"
        },
        {
          "resource": "rl4-spinning-up-policy-optimization",
          "title": "Part 3: Intro to Policy Optimization",
          "url": "https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro3.html#deriving-the-simplest-policy-gradient",
          "locator": "Deriving the Simplest Policy Gradient, facts 1 to 5 and Derivation for Basic Policy Gradient",
          "depth": "Stop before Implementing the Simplest Policy Gradient."
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "Use the trajectory contract from Trajectories, episodes, horizons, discounting, termination and truncation. Include the initial-state law, each policy factor and each transition factor.",
        "For a fixed environment and parameter-independent reward, only policy factors contribute to the trajectory score. Differentiability, fixed support and a valid interchange of derivative and expectation are assumptions, not consequences of sampling.",
        "The score has mean zero: \\(\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim p_\\theta}[\\nabla_\\theta\\log p_\\theta(\\tau)]=0\\). Multiplying it by return creates the learning signal."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Closed-book, derive \\(\\nabla_\\theta J=\\mathbb{E}[R(\\tau)\\sum_t\\nabla_\\theta\\log\\pi_\\theta(a_t\\mid s_t)]\\). Check a one-step Bernoulli policy with \\(p=\\operatorname{sigmoid}(\\theta)=1/4\\), reward 2 for action 1 and -1 for action 0: \\(J=3p-1=-1/4\\) and \\(dJ/d\\theta=3p(1-p)=9/16\\). Enumerate both actions and recover the same gradient from their scores.",
      "traps": [
        "Differentiating a sampled reward as if the sampled action were a differentiable path through the environment. The score-function estimator differentiates the sampling law instead.",
        "Dropping a reward or transition derivative when those functions actually share policy parameters. The displayed estimator assumes they do not.",
        "Summing action probabilities rather than log probabilities along a trajectory. Independent conditional factors multiply; their logs add."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Why can an unknown transition model disappear from the score?",
          "a": "Its log derivative is zero when the transition law has no policy parameters. Its effect remains in the sampled state distribution and rewards."
        },
        {
          "q": "What changes if the return explicitly depends on policy parameters?",
          "a": "Add \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\nabla_\\theta R_\\theta(\\tau)]\\) to the likelihood-ratio term, under the same differentiation assumptions."
        },
        {
          "q": "Does an action-dependent reward require a differentiable reward function?",
          "a": "No. Dependence on a sampled action is already handled by the trajectory likelihood. Explicit dependence on policy parameters is a separate issue."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [],
      "practiceNote": "The section mini-project covers this: the policylab exact-enumeration policy-gradient oracle.",
      "stopWhen": "You derive the trajectory score without notes and match the one-step gradient by direct differentiation and exact enumeration.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "The lecture and Spinning Up supply the full derivation; the mini-project supplies an independent gradient oracle."
      }
    },
